\f1\fs22 The next morning, goons with particle pistols marched the Three Sisters in pyjamas out of the townhouse, up the road to the main house. The air was dry, and sun was high in the sky; it seemed to burn more sharply than in the city. The discomfort made the distance much longer than when they\'92d be driven down after dark. The main building loomed ever more ominously, asserting again the technical wizardry of its Warlord Cinderella aggressiveness. Its windows shimmered with smoky-glass reflections of the surrounding bushland and appeared to shimmer out of existence entirely if they looked at it too hard,  leaving the five towers that were a constantly changing superimposition of Islamic minarets of metal ivy spiralling towards the sky and towering old-growth trees.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As the company rounded the last curve in the road, a massive portico came into view which sat at the top of a creamy-white onyx spiral staircase. The hearts of the Three Sisters lifted at the magnificence of the staircase, expecting to rise up to where they\'92d sort out this silly issue with Eloise. Instead, their guards led them down a narrow block of stones stairs within sight of their hoped-for ascendence, through a heavy metal door that gave entrance to a practical hallway you might see in a hospital.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Then a stairway down again.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And down again, until they finally reached another hallway from which they turned left and went down again.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah was begin to think they\'92d descend all the way to the floor of the Jamison Valley\'97or hell, whichever came first. Instead, they were ushered into what she could only surmise, judging by the single stainless steel bunk and the single, less stainless toilet, was a holding cell. The blunt, heavy door swung shut behind them. They heard sounds that recalled large locks and bolts fastening.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Via her tablet, Eloise watched the three women slump onto the Spartan bunk. With elbows on thighs they held their heads in the hands. Only Sarah moved; she brushed her trenchantly blonde hair over her shoulder, which caused the image on Eloise\'92s tablet to momentarily flare.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise expected they\'92d be scratching and fighting within minutes\'97verbally, at least, if not bodily. But they sat there motionless, shoulder to shoulder with heads in the hands to within a second of Eloise\'92s patience. Until Hayley looked up and said, \'91I\'92m really sorry.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What?\'92 Sarah asked, looking up and putting her hand on Hayley\'92s shoulder. \'91No \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s not your fault,\'92 Kathy said, trying to peer at Hayley through Sarah\'92s hair, then deciding to reach across and put a hand on Hayley\'92s knee. \'91I got you started, with that rant about no free lunches. At lunch time.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise tried to restore her eyebrow to normal positioning, and failed. So she put the tablet down and went about other business while listening for any signs of an eruption between the captives. After thirty minutes, she flicked a switch on a desk console and said, \'91Okay, bring them up.\'92
